co={(r['model'],r['term']):r for r in m['coefficients']}
header=f'''<header class="report-header"><span class="eyebrow">SURGO × UNIVERSITY OF MANITOBA · SINDH</span><h1>Demand, services and modern contraceptive use</h1><p>Linked district-level service environments and women’s own reported concerns help identify where different kinds of support may be needed. This report follows the Lagos analysis: coverage, use and intention, joint conditions, district planning profiles, adjusted modern-use models and a PCA sensitivity.</p><p><strong>Initial evidence:</strong> higher personal demand concern scores are associated with lower modern use. The selected supply score has an uncertain adjusted association. These findings support joint investigation of reported concerns and service gaps; they do not establish causal binding constraints.</p><nav><a href="#coverage">1 · Coverage</a><a href="#use">2 · Use and intention</a><a href="#joint">3 · Joint conditions</a><a href="#models">4 · Adjusted models</a><a href="#definitions">Definitions and checks</a><a href="#pca">Appendix · PCA</a></nav></header>'''
coverage=f'''<section id="coverage"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="eyebrow">01 · DATA COVERAGE</span><h2>Women and facilities are linked by district</h2></div></div><p class="intro">All 30 surveyed districts have a facility profile in the existing administrative-area join. The joined file retains all 10,364 source rows. Weighted women’s estimates use 9,926 consenting women with positive weights; 426 nonconsenting records and 12 consenting women without weights are outside estimation.</p><p class="model-note"><strong>Coverage versus completeness:</strong> Tando Allah Yar has 26 included facilities, but no valid training or counselor responses in this join. Its 41 women in need remain in overview results; its supply score is unassessed and its women cannot enter the common complete-supply model sample.</p><div class="metrics"><div class="metric"><b>30</b><span>Surveyed districts with facility profiles</span></div><div class="metric"><b>9,926</b><span>Consenting women with positive weights</span></div><div class="metric"><b>3,274</b><span>Women in the agreed derived need group</span></div><div class="metric"><b>3,427</b><span>Facilities mapped by the source crosswalk</span></div></div>{coverage_legend}{map_svg('coverage')}<p class="model-note"><strong>One-sided coverage:</strong> none. All 30 mapped survey districts have both sources. Missing component responses, such as Tando Allah Yar’s training and counselor values, do not mean the district has no facility data.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('district_data_coverage.csv','District data-coverage counts')}</div><p class="footnote">Dots mark district summary locations, not actual facility coordinates. Under this source-crosswalk version, 881 of the 4,308 Sindh facilities lack a mapped district because their source district_name is blank; they are not silently added from the separate completed-geography alternative. Boundaries are from the <a href="https://atlas.geospatial.wfp.org/server/rest/services/Hosted/pak_bnd_adm3district_aha_usda/FeatureServer">WFP Geoportal</a>; current display boundaries may differ from the survey-period geography. The explicit 30-district name crosswalk is verified.</p></section>'''
use=f'''<section id="use"><span class="eyebrow">02 · USE, INTENTION AND METHOD CHOICE</span><h2>How women in the agreed need group are distributed</h2><div class="report-warning"><strong>Need definition:</strong> use <b>fpn2_derived = 1</b> from the supplied Sindh pipeline, matched by woman ID. It reconstructs the Pakistan questionnaire’s current-need rule; original fpn2 is 95 throughout. It retains the existing SA1=95/97 exclusion policy and does not add the pipeline’s permanent-protection cases. This definition is used consistently from this section onward.</div>{bar_chart()}<p class="footnote">Every bar and sub-bar uses the same denominator: all {n:,} women in the derived need group. Percentages use Normalizedweights; n is an unweighted respondent count. Whiskers are 95% logit intervals from the available sampling-cluster design using clusterCode; original sampling strata and FPC are unavailable. The modern/traditional and preference bars overlap with the current-user bar and do not sum to 100%. Among current users, 4 have an unresolved modern/traditional classification and 115 have another or unresolved initiation-preference response. *Preference refers to the method wanted at initiation; the alternative method type is not established. Intention is to use any method at any future time, not specifically modern use or use within 12 months.</p>{note(f'<strong>Initial pattern.</strong> {100*bars["user"]["estimate"]:.1f}% are confirmed current users and {100*bars["modern"]["estimate"]:.1f}% confirmed modern users. {100*bars["intender"]["estimate"]:.1f}% are nonusers with stated intention, and {100*bars["no_intent"]["estimate"]:.1f}% nonusers without stated intention. The {bars["unresolved"]["n"]} unresolved use/intention records remain visible rather than being assigned to a group.')}<div class="table-heading">{download('use_distribution.csv','Use and intention estimates')}{download('district_use_rates.csv','District use estimates')}</div></section>'''
threshold=cuts['zscore']
score_walkthrough=f"""<details open class="model-detail" id="classification-method"><summary>How the map scores and thresholds are constructed</summary><ol class="score-steps"><li><b>Summarize each district.</b> Demand inputs are six SURGO-weighted concern percentages among valid women in the agreed need group. Supply inputs are the six unweighted facility summaries already attached by district, with their original valid-response denominators.</li><li><b>Standardize each component across districts.</b> Calculate (district value − mean of valid district values) ÷ sample standard deviation. Each district contributes equally to this reference, regardless of population or respondent count. All demand inputs run higher = more concern; all raw supply inputs run higher = stronger provision.</li><li><b>Average all six component z-scores on each side.</b> This gives a district demand score and a district supply-readiness score. All six components are required. These are area scores for the map; the regressions use each woman's personal demand score. The map uses z-score averages, not PCA or a preliminary ranking.</li><li><b>Compare each score with its own median.</b> The median of 30 observed demand scores is <b>{threshold['demand_cutoff']:.6f}</b>; flag demand strictly above it. The median of 29 complete supply-readiness scores is <b>{threshold['supply_cutoff']:.6f}</b>; flag supply strictly below it. Exact ties are unflagged. Equivalently, a sign-reversed supply-risk score is flagged strictly above <b>{-threshold['supply_cutoff']:.6f}</b>.</li></ol><p>Both flags → <b>Both</b>; demand alone → <b>Demand only</b>; supply alone → <b>Supply only</b>; neither → <b>Neither flagged</b>. A missing supply score gives <b>Supply unassessed</b>, not “Neither.” These are relative planning profiles, not causal diagnoses or validated adequacy standards. Small-sample flags do not change the scores, thresholds or categories.</p></details>"""
small_list=', '.join(titlecase(rates[g]['name'])+' (n='+str(rates[g]['n'])+')' for g in sorted(small_areas,key=lambda g:rates[g]['n']))
sample_note=f"""<p class="footnote"><b>† Small need-group sample:</b> fewer than 50 unweighted women. Flagged districts: {esc(small_list)}. These caution flags do not establish precision or power; weights, clustering and each indicator's valid denominator also matter. The 50-case boundary is informed by <a href="https://github.com/DHSProgram/DHS-Indicators-R/blob/main/Readme.md">DHS reporting conventions</a>, which flag estimates based on 25–49 cases and suppress those below 25. No district total here is below 25, and no estimates or districts are suppressed.</p>"""
joint=f'''<section id="joint"><span class="eyebrow">03 · JOINT DEMAND AND SUPPLY CONDITIONS</span><h2>Reported concerns alongside recorded services</h2><p class="intro">Read across each district: weighted personal concerns among women in need on the left, and unweighted district facility summaries on the right. This shows both sides of the service environment together without requiring an exact one-to-one counterpart for every reported concern.</p>{heatmap()}<p class="footnote">Darker teal means more reported concern. Darker orange means weaker provision; the displayed supply percentages all run higher = stronger provision. Hover cells for valid-response denominators. The Sindh row pools women and mapped facilities. Gray means no valid response, not zero provision. Training and counselor measures have particularly sparse reporting. The LARC stock measure uses at least one LARC; the original three-LARC measure cannot be standardized in Sindh. † District need-group sample below 50 women.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('heatmap_values_and_denominators.csv','Heatmap values and denominators')}{download('demand_missingness_by_district.csv','Demand response coverage')}</div><div class="wide-break"></div><h3>How district classifications compare across scoring approaches</h3>{table(['District']+method_names,scorerows)}{methodexplanations}<p class="footnote">Component references use valid district values. Demand scores are available in all 30 districts; complete supply scores in 29. Each final-score median is calculated from the scores available on that side. Every component receives equal weight; exact median ties are unflagged. Median count requires all six observed components and its 4-of-6 rule. Missing supply components are not zero-filled or averaged over a reduced set.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('classification_comparison.csv','Five-method comparison')}{download('classification_thresholds.csv','Exact thresholds')}{download('component_score_trace.csv','Component transformations')}</div><div class="wide-break"></div><h3>District planning profiles · z-score average</h3>{score_walkthrough}<div class="rule-strip"><span>Demand flagged above <b>{threshold['demand_cutoff']:.4f}</b>.</span><span>Supply readiness flagged below <b>{threshold['supply_cutoff']:.4f}</b>.</span><span>These are relative district thresholds, not clinical or service-adequacy standards.</span></div>{legend}{map_svg('classification')}<p class="footnote">Hover for weighted any-method and modern-method use among women in the agreed need group. Tando Allah Yar’s training and counseling values are missing, so its supply classification is unassessed. “Neither flagged” does not establish adequate services or an absence of barriers.</p>{sample_note}<div class="table-heading">{download('district_sample_flags.csv','District sample-size flags')}</div><h3>District planning priorities</h3>{table(['District','Demand challenges','Top three demand components','Supply challenges','Top three service weaknesses'],priorityrows)}<p class="footnote">Top demand components have the largest component z-scores. Top service weaknesses have the smallest favorable component z-scores. Raw percentages are shown to support interpretation. Lists appear only for a flagged side; they rank recorded conditions rather than estimating which intervention will have the largest effect.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('planning_priorities.csv','Planning priorities')}{download('all_method_scores_and_classifications.csv','All scores and categories')}</div></section>'''
model=f'<section id="models"><span class="eyebrow">04 · ADJUSTED ASSOCIATIONS</span>'+models_section(m,'models_zscore')+'</section>'

defs_d=[['Healthcare permission barriers','HS7c = 1 versus 2. General permission to seek healthcare; not an FP-specific decision-control scale.'],['Anticipated social stigma','PB7b/c = 1 or 2 on either item. A known positive establishes concern; zero requires all valid negatives; otherwise missing.'],['Health / side-effect beliefs','PB2a–c: concerns about health problems, reduced sexual desire or deformed babies. Any item coded 1/2 establishes concern; all valid 3/4 establish zero. Bleeding and fertility are separate.'],['Future-fertility concerns','Any MP4_1–6 selected: condom, injectable, pill, ECP, IUD or implant believed to affect later childbearing. Require coherent 0/1 answers across MP4_1–9, at least one selection or explicit none, and no none-plus-selection contradiction. Sterilization items are excluded.'],['Low method knowledge · display label','MC5a/b = 3/4 on either item: low confidence choosing a method or managing side effects/switching. This is self-reported confidence, not factual knowledge.'],['Opposes family planning','FP1 = 3/4 versus 1/2. Codes for not knowing about FP, no opinion or skipped remain unresolved.']]
defs_s=[['Short + long-acting offering','Mean of UoM one_larc_3_sa_offer, copied as share_offer_larc_and_3_sa. Percentage meeting the original ≥1 long-acting plus ≥3 short-acting product/category rule.'],['Free-product provision','Mean of valid facility p_free_prod values. Average facility-level share of recorded products free; not the percentage of facilities offering something free.'],['Trained-provider share','Mean of valid p_train facility proportions using the original UoM personnel/topic eligibility. Not the percentage of facilities with a trained provider. Sparse responses do not establish provision among unreported facilities.'],['FP counselor availability','Q702 Yes/(Yes+No), copied as share_fp_counselor. Unknown responses excluded, coverage reported. Availability is not observed counseling receipt or quality.'],['At least one LARC in stock','Mean of one_larc_stockin, copied as share_stocked_1plus_larc. Share meeting the original at-least-one long-acting product-in-stock rule. Replaces the country-wide constant three-LARC indicator in this report only.'],['Most-popular product in stock','n_mp_in_stock_true / (true + false). Percentage among facilities with known status, for each facility’s own most-popular product. This is not a woman’s preferred method.']]
samplerows=[[r['model'],f'{r["n"]:,}',f'{r["events"]:,}',str(r['parameters']),f'{r["max_vif"]:.2f}'] for r in m['samples']]
tests_missing=list(csv.DictReader((root/'models_zscore/model_missingness.csv').open()))
coverage_rows=[]
for g in areas:
    coverage_rows.append([esc(titlecase(rates[g]['name'])),str(rates[g]['n']),str(cov[g]['facilities'])]+[str(hm[g,'Supply',k]['n_valid']) for k in ['training','counselor','popular_stock']])
correlations=list(csv.DictReader((root/'checks/component_correlations.csv').open()))
correlation_rows=[]
for side in ['Demand','Supply']:
    pairs=sorted([r for r in correlations if r['side']==side and r['item1']<r['item2']],key=lambda r:abs(float(r['correlation'])),reverse=True)[:3]
    correlation_rows += [[side,esc(labels[r['item1']]),esc(labels[r['item2']]),f'{float(r["correlation"]):+.3f}',r['n_pair']] for r in pairs]
defs=f'''<section id="definitions"><span class="eyebrow">METHODS, DEFINITIONS AND CHECKS</span><h2>What the measures represent</h2><details open class="model-detail"><summary>Demand indicators</summary>{table(['Indicator','Source, coding and interpretation'],defs_d)}</details><details open class="model-detail"><summary>Supply indicators</summary>{table(['Indicator','Calculation and interpretation'],defs_s)}</details><details class="model-detail"><summary>Need reconstruction and its coding qualification</summary><p>The supplied fpn2 variable is coded 95 on all 10,364 source rows. The authorized fpn2_derived flag excludes never having sex, SA1 codes 5/6/95/97, pregnancy, wanting a child within a year, current breastfeeding, postpartum amenorrhea, hysterectomy, definite menopause or reporting inability to become pregnant. Other missing item responses are not automatically exclusions under that upstream implementation. This is the Pakistan instrument-based reconstruction, not the broader nonuser segmentation population described in a separate memo, and not the DHS unmet-need algorithm.</p><p>Independent reconstruction agrees on every eligible weighted respondent. The chosen flag gives 3,274 women in need (32.9% weighted among 9,926 consenting positive-weight women). The later need_n variable adds 369 permanent-protection cases and is not used. SA1=95/97 do not establish sexual inactivity: they remain excluded solely to reproduce the authorized flag. Excluding only actual SA1 codes 5/6 would give 3,346 women, 72 more; this report does not silently make that change.</p></details><details class="model-detail"><summary>Weights, available sampling design and model diagnostics</summary><p>Use final <b>Normalizedweights</b>. The available sampling design uses the recorded <b>clusterCode</b> field ({AUD['survey_psus']} clusters among consenting positive-weight women); district membership is verified to be unique within each cluster. The rb0e copy exactly matches clusterCode after single-precision rounding, which collapses some large IDs; the unrounded clusterCode is retained. Overview confidence intervals use this available cluster design after full-frame construction and domain subsetting. Original sampling strata, FPC and full design documentation remain unavailable, so a complete original-design reconstruction cannot be claimed.</p><p>Primary regression uncertainty is CR2 clustered by district to account for shared contextual supply exposure, with coefficient-specific Satterthwaite degrees of freedom. Corresponding sampling-cluster survey-GLM coefficients and intervals are exported as an inference sensitivity. Point estimates agree numerically. Both choices retain the original weights; no random effects or interactions are fitted.</p>{table(['Model','Women','Modern users','Parameters','Maximum VIF'],samplerows)}<p>All five models converge, have full-rank designs and finite coefficients. Maximum VIF is {max(r['max_vif'] for r in m['samples']):.2f}. The common sample contains {model_n:,} women ({100*MA['eligible_weight_share']:.1f}% of the need-group survey weight). Tando Allah Yar contributes 41 women to the need-group overview but no common-model cases because its complete supply score is undefined. Remaining exclusions reflect unresolved demand/outcome/covariate values; no women are removed from the source join.</p><p>Covariates are age and age², Pakistan-specific education categories (primary or less; middle/secondary; tertiary/professional), and fertility goal. Partnership is constant among the modeled women, so it cannot enter as an identifiable coefficient. Urban/rural is absent in the Sindh women file and is not inferred from facilities.</p><p>Verification reproduced every original raw survey column, all 180 facility-to-joined supply components, all ten regression fits and district covariance matrices, and the PCA projections independently using weighted singular-value decomposition. The full audit checklist is downloadable below.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('checks/audit_checks.csv','Independent verification checklist')}</div><p>Supply still has only 29 distinct complete district values in the models. The joint z-score supply coefficient has {co['score_joint','supply_risk']['df']:.1f} effective degrees of freedom. This limits inference despite the woman count. No formal power guarantee, causal binding-constraint identification, or attribution of nonuse is made. Intervals condition on estimated scores. The data are cross-sectional, and attitudes may also reflect current contraceptive experience.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('models_zscore/model_samples.csv','Sample and convergence')}{download('models_zscore/model_missingness.csv','Model missingness')}{download('models_zscore/model_vifs.csv','Weighted VIF')}{download('models_zscore/leave_one_district_out.csv','District-deletion checks')}{download('models_zscore/sampling_cluster_model_sensitivity.csv','Sampling-cluster inference sensitivity')}{download('models_zscore/individual_demand_score_reference.csv','Demand score means and SDs')}</div></details><details class="model-detail"><summary>Component overlap and correlations</summary>{table(['Side','Component 1','Component 2','Correlation','Paired records'],correlation_rows)}<p>Demand correlations use individual women in the need group with final SURGO weights and valid pairs. Supply correlations use one unweighted row per district with valid pairs. Method offering and at-least-one-LARC stock are highly correlated (r = 0.961). They enter the six-component index as separate recorded features, so equal weighting emphasizes related availability information. This is a composite-design limitation, not evidence that these are independent supply domains. The score models do not estimate six separate supply coefficients. PCA captures their shared pattern but does not turn statistical weights into programmatic importance.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('checks/component_correlations.csv','All component correlations and pair counts')}</div></details><details class="model-detail"><summary>Facility reporting coverage and join limitations</summary>{table(['District','Women in need','Mapped facilities','Training responses','Counselor responses','Known popular-stock status'],coverage_rows)}<p>These counts are component-specific valid responses, not interchangeable denominators. Only {sum(hm[g,'Supply','training']['n_valid'] for g in areas):,} mapped facilities report valid training proportions and {sum(hm[g,'Supply','counselor']['n_valid'] for g in areas):,} valid counselor responses. The original UoM definitions are preserved; missing responses do not mean no service. Facilities without source-crosswalk districts are excluded from district profiles, and the completed-geography alternative has not been substituted.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('supply_input_reconciliation.csv','All 180 supply-input checks')}{download('district_supply_profiles.csv','District supply profiles')}{download('map_name_crosswalk.csv','Boundary name crosswalk')}</div></details></section>'''

loadingrows=[[r['side'],esc(labels[r['variable']]),f'{r["loading"]:+.3f}'] for r in pc['loadings'] if r['PC']==1]
varrows=[[r['side'],f'PC{r["PC"]}',f'{r["variance_pct"]:.1f}%',f'{r["cumulative_pct"]:.1f}%'] for r in pc['variance']]
pca=f'''<section id="pca"><span class="eyebrow">APPENDIX · PCA SENSITIVITY</span><h2>Alternative scores from the same personal and district inputs</h2><p class="intro">Separate weighted correlation PCAs summarize the six personal demand indicators and the six assigned district supply measures. Demand PCA uses {pc['audit']['demand_PCA_n']:,} complete women in the need group. Supply PCA uses {pc['audit']['supply_PCA_women_n']:,} women with complete assigned supply values, equivalent to {pc['audit']['supply_profiles']} district profiles weighted by their represented women’s summed SURGO weights. These are not facility sampling weights.</p><div class="report-warning"><strong>Important Sindh difference:</strong> demand PC1 assigns a negative coefficient to low method confidence. It therefore describes a <b>contrast among concerns</b>, not a score that necessarily increases whenever any concern increases. It is labeled “Demand pattern score (PCA).” The primary equal-weight demand risk score remains monotonic in all six concerns. PCA weights describe input variation, not causal importance for contraceptive use.</div>{figure('pca_appendix/01_pca_loadings.svg','Signed PCA scoring coefficients for six individual demand and six district supply inputs.')}{note(f'<strong>What PCA retains.</strong> Demand PC1 captures {pc["audit"]["demand_PC1_variance"]:.1f}% of demand variation; supply PC1 captures {pc["audit"]["supply_PC1_variance"]:.1f}%. Demand PC1 emphasizes healthcare permission and stigma, with confidence entering in the opposite direction. Supply PC1 has positive coefficients on all six favorable measures and is negated for model use.')}{figure('pca_appendix/02_pca_variance.svg','Variance retained across demand and supply principal components and exploratory permutation benchmarks.')}<details class="model-detail"><summary>PCA coefficients, dimensionality and stability</summary>{table(['Side','Input','PC1 coefficient'],loadingrows)}{table(['Side','Component','Variance','Cumulative'],varrows)}<p>PC1 is prespecified as a sensitivity, without using the outcome to choose coefficients. Its units differ from average-z units; coefficient magnitudes across scoring methods are not directly comparable. The appendix does not create PCA-based district categories. Exploratory 2,000-permutation checks and leave-one-district-out PCA refits are available; these are construction diagnostics, not full survey-design significance tests. Regression intervals do not propagate PCA-estimation uncertainty.</p><div class="table-heading">{download('pca_appendix/pca_loadings.csv','All PCA coefficients')}{download('pca_appendix/pca_centers_scales.csv','PCA centers and scales')}{download('pca_appendix/pca_variance.csv','Variance and permutation checks')}{download('pca_appendix/pca_leave_one_district_out.csv','PCA district-deletion checks')}</div></details>{models_section(pm,'pca_appendix/models',pca=True)}<p class="footnote">Method references: <a href="https://knowledge-for-policy.ec.europa.eu/composite-indicators/toolkit_en/navigation-page/10-step-guide_en/step-5-normalisation_en">JRC normalization</a>; <a href="https://knowledge-for-policy.ec.europa.eu/composite-indicators/toolkit_en/navigation-page/10-step-guide_en/step-6-weighting_en">JRC weighting/PCA</a>; <a href="https://jepusto.github.io/clubSandwich/reference/vcovCR.glm.html">CR2 inference for weighted GLMs</a>. All figures are estimates from the Sindh data, not illustrative or borrowed Lagos results.</p></section>'''

js='''const DATA=__DATA__;const tip=document.querySelector('.tooltip');
function hide(){tip.classList.remove('show')}
function move(e){let x=e.clientX+17,y=e.clientY+17;if(x+tip.offsetWidth>innerWidth-10)x=e.clientX-tip.offsetWidth-17;if(y+tip.offsetHeight>innerHeight-10)y=e.clientY-tip.offsetHeight-17;tip.style.left=Math.max(10,x)+'px';tip.style.top=Math.max(10,y)+'px'}
function show(el,e){const d=DATA[el.dataset.key];if(!d)return;tip.innerHTML='<strong>'+d.name+'</strong><p>'+(el.dataset.mode==='coverage'?d.coverage_label:d.category)+'</p><div class="two"><div><b>'+d.cpr.toFixed(1)+'%</b><br><small>Any-method use</small></div><div><b>'+d.mcpr.toFixed(1)+'%</b><br><small>Modern use</small></div></div><p>Among '+d.n+' women in the agreed need group.</p><p>'+d.women+' consenting weighted respondents · '+d.facilities+' mapped facility records</p>'+(el.dataset.mode==='classification'?'<p>Demand score: '+d.demand.toFixed(3)+'<br>Supply readiness: '+(d.supply==null?'Unassessed':d.supply.toFixed(3))+'</p>':'')+(d.small_sample?'<p class="sample-alert">† Small need-group sample: n='+d.n+' (below 50). Interpret district estimates and categories cautiously.</p>':'')+'<small>Weighted survey estimates; district-level facility context.</small>';tip.classList.add('show');move(e)}
document.querySelectorAll('[data-key][data-mode]').forEach(el=>{el.addEventListener('pointerenter',e=>show(el,e));el.addEventListener('pointermove',move);el.addEventListener('pointerleave',hide);el.addEventListener('focus',()=>{let r=el.getBoundingClientRect();show(el,{clientX:r.x+r.width/2,clientY:r.y+r.height/2})});el.addEventListener('blur',hide);el.addEventListener('click',e=>show(el,e))});document.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(e.key==='Escape')hide()});
'''.replace('__DATA__',json.dumps(DATA,separators=(',',':')))
page='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Sindh_analysis | SURGO × Manitoba</title><style>'+css+'</style></head><body>'+header+'<main>'+coverage+use+joint+model+defs+pca+'</main><div class="tooltip" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div><footer style="max-width:1430px;margin:20px auto 35px;padding:0 30px;color:#6a8290;font-size:12px">Sindh · district-level linkage · instrument-based derived need · initial cross-sectional evidence.</footer><script>'+js+'</script></body></html>'
output.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True);output.write_text(page)
print('Rendered',output.name,'with 30 districts, weighted need-group summaries, five classification methods and ten modern-use model specifications.')
